3.1 Regional Cold-Chain Perishability & Gujarat District Logistics

Emergency blood logistics are very tough in real life  . Blood units is getting spoiled quickly if it are not kept cool properly . Gujarat are spanning across 34 administrative districts  . These is ranging from busy city like Ahmedabad to quiet rural borders  . There are peripheral tribal and border regions that requires careful planning , like Dang , Kutch , and newly created Vav-Tharad district  . In blood banking , time are being the biggest enemy of patients  . Central Drugs Standard Control Organization  (CDSCO) rules sets strict storage temprature and shelf lives for bags . Whole blood and PRBC units stays safe for 35 days in CPDA-1 at +2°C to +6°C  . Platelets is far more delicate than red cells  . They lasts only 5 days at 20°C to 24°C under continuous gentle agitations  . Fresh Frozen Plasma are needing -30°C storage for to last one full year  .

Hospitals are facing three very big operational problems during triage :
• Supply Disparities: City trauma center in urban districts experiences rapid inventory loss , while remote district hospitals holds surplus units that are risking to expire  .
• Depot Silos: Hospitals lacks real-time insight into nearby blood bank stock levels  . Staffs wastes precious minutes by making frantic phone calls  .
• Dispatch Races: Simultaneous emergencies leads to two hospitals fighting for the exact same rare blood unit  .

3.2 3-Tier Multi-Data-Structure Pipeline Architecture

We has solved this problem with a clean 3-tier pipeline  . Four fast data structures works together nicely :
1. Client Tier: It take emergency input directly from hospital ICU ward  . A client-side Separate Chaining Hash Table give instant city autocomplete across 316+ Gujarat towns  .
2. Broker Tier: A FIFO queue are buffering incoming WebSocket requests  . It assign atomic tokens and prevent race conditions  .
3. Matching Tier: An In-Memory 3D Matrix are finding donors in O(1) time  . An embedded Binary Max-Heap pick the top donor in O(log N) time  .

4. Data Structure 1: In-Memory 3D Static Matrix Indexing

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

4.1 Dimensional Coordinates & Index Math

In BloodLink , we is avoiding slow database scans completely  . Disk queries takes 15ms or even more  . That are being far too slow for trauma patients  . Instead , BloodLink maintain a 3D Static Matrix right inside memory  . Speed are our main goal here  . We does not query MySQL database during live emergency triage  . We is splitting donor lookup into three simple axes :
• Dimension 1 (City Axis C): Dimension 1 map each recognized Gujarat district to a direct array key  (e.g. Ahmedabad , Gandhinagar , Surat , Vav-Tharad)  .
• Dimension 2 (Blood Group Axis G): It index the 8 standard ABO/Rh blood group : A+ , A- , B+ , B- , AB+ , AB- , O+ , and O-  .
• Dimension 3 (Max-Heap Instance H): Each cell within the grid host an isolated instance of Binary Max-Heap filled with active voluntary donors  .

4.2 Mathematical Formalism & Direct Array Access

We is treating our donor pool as an array matrix M(c, g)  . Here c are the city index from 0 to 33 , and g are the blood group index from 0 to 7  . The indexing function f(c, g) compute the exact memory offset directly by using a fixed step coeffecient :
$$\text{Offset}(M_{c, g}) = (c \times 8 + g) \times \text{sizeof}(\text{Pointer})$$
Because JavaScript objects resolves direct keys in constant time , looking up the right Max-Heap are taking pure O(1) time :
$$T_{\text{lookup}}(\text{city}, \text{bg}) = O(1)$$
We does not scan even a single extra record  . The lookup are completely instant  .

4.3 Runtime Invariants & Atomic Updates

▪ O(1) Registration Invariant: When a donor are registering , the server save their record and <b>immediately push</b> a reference into matching heap without touching unrelated cities  .

▪ Cell Isolation Guarantee: Because each <b>leaf cell operate</b> independently , updating one district are never requiring any locking on adjacent cities or other blood groups  .

▪ Zero Table Scanning: The system are guaranteeing zero linear search loops  ($O(N)$) during emergency matching  . Triage are remaining fast even under heavy hospital loads  .

4.4 Memory Layout & Spatial Cache Locality

Memory layout are mattering a lot for achieving high speed  . The entire donor index reside directly in Node.js heap memory  . With 34 districts and 8 blood groups , our matrix are having a fixed number of cells :
$$\text{Total Active Cells} = 34 \times 8 = 272 \text{ leaf MaxHeaps}$$
The pointer overhead for 34 districts and 8 blood groups are strictly bounded in RAM  . Even with 10,000 donors across Gujarat , the entire matrix are consuming less than 18 Megabytes  . Because array nodes sits next to each other in memory , modern CPU caches utilizes prefetching for to load elements into fast L1/L2 cache  . This are providing maximum computational efficency and avoid garbage collection pauses completely  .

5. Data Structure 2: Binary Max-Heap Priority Queue

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

5.1 Complete Binary Tree Array Index Math

Finding just any donor are not being enough for patients  . We must find the best donor first  . In life-critical trauma , simply finding any compatible donor are insufficient  . We is organizing each donor pool as a heirarchical Complete Binary Tree  . A Complete Binary Tree are filled level by level from left to right side  . Because of this shape , we is storing it inside a flat array  . We does not need heavy node pointers  . Parent-child relationships in this flat array is computed via fast arithmetic :
$$\text{Parent}(i) = \left\lfloor \frac{i - 1}{2} \right\rfloor, \quad \text{Left}(i) = 2i + 1, \quad \text{Right}(i) = 2i + 2$$
The heap property dictate that a parent node must always score higher than or equal to its children nodes :
$$\text{Heap}[\text{Parent}(i)].\text{priority} \ge \text{Heap}[i].\text{priority}$$

5.2 Multi-Factor Clinical Prioritization Scoring Formula

We is ranking donors using clinical criteria  . Multi-factor clinical scoring incorporate three key parameters :
$$P(D, R) = S_{\text{serology}}(D, R) + W_{\text{age}}(D) + B_{\text{first\_time}}(D)$$
Here are how the scoring are working in practice :
• Exact Serological Match (+100 pts): Donor blood group match patient type identically  (e.g. A+ to A+)  .
• Compatible Match (+60 pts): Universal donors like O- for positive blood recipients  .
• Age Proximity Bonus (0 to 20 pts): Donors near peak physical fitness  (age 25) receive top points : $20 - |\text{age} - 25|$  .
• First-Time Bonus (+20 pts): It encourage new voluntary donors for to participate  .
The most ready donor are always sitting right at array index 0  .

5.3 Core Algorithmic Operations (BubbleUp & SinkDown)

Our heap are relying on two main rebalancing operations  . Their repeated occurrance during emergency processing are guaranteeing optimal tree shape :
• Bubble-Up ($O(\log N)$): When a new donor sign up , the insert() method append them to the end of the array  . The bubbleUp algorithm compare the new child with its parent  . If the child have a higher score , they swaps  . This traversal repeat until order hold  .
• Sink-Down ($O(\log N)$): For to extract the top donor , the extractMax method remove the root  . We is moving the last donor to the top and sift down  . If either child have a higher priority score , we is swapping with the larger child  .

5.4 Top-K Extraction & Emergency Dispatch Workflow

In emergencies , the first donor might not answer their phone  . That are being a very real risk  . So we is extracting the top 3 candidates at once  . Our `getTopK(3)` algorithm clone the root elements quickly  . The persistent heap instance that remain in memory are staying completely untouched  . We is extracting candidates in $O(k \log N)$ time without corrupting our master pool  .

6. Data Structure 3: FIFO Request Queue & WebSocket Triage Broker

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

6.1 Formal Queue Invariants & Arrival-Order Discipline

During major accidents or highway disasters , multiple hospitals submits requests at once  . Unbuffered requests causes chaos and dropped connections  . In life-critical transfusoin medicine , fair queuing are very vital  . A First-In-First-Out queue enforce strict arrival order  . The queue maintain two simple pointers : head and tail  . We is using two main operations :
• Enqueue ($O(1)$): It add an incoming request payload to the tail  .
• Dequeue ($O(1)$): It remove the oldest pending request from the head  .
This simple discipline are guaranteeing fairness  . Urgent requests are processed in exact arrival order  .

6.2 Atomic Token Generation & Auditing Pipeline

Every incoming requisition receive a unique token ID :
$$\text{Token} = \text{TOKEN-}[\text{Timestamp}]_{10\dots 13}\text{-}[\text{Sequence}]_{3}$$
The atomic counter guarantee unique token numbering across asynchronous event loops  . The workflow are simple and clean :
1. A hospital submit a requisition via WebSockets  .
2. The server generate an atomic token like `TOKEN-1603-512` in under 2ms  .
3. The hospital screen show the confirmed token right away  .
4. The request sit in queue until our matching engine execute  .

6.3 Concurrency Control & Race Condition Mitigation

Concurrency bugs can kill patients easily  . If two ICUs requests the last unit of rare O- blood at the exact same second , simultaneous requests causes race conditions  . Both hospitals might get promised the same unit  . That lead to disaster  . Our FIFO queue broker serialize all triage operations into a single execution sequence  . Each matching run execute to completion in a synhronous step before the next ticket start  . This are completely eliminating double-allocation of scarce blood reserves  .

6.4 Event-Driven WebSocket Protocol & Targeted Emission

The WebSocket transport layer deliver fast updates directly to hospital consoles  . Hospital clients connects through Socket.io channels over persistent TCP connections  . The communication protocol follow four clean steps :
• Connect: Hospital connect and get a socket ID  .
• Request: ICU send blood group , units , and hospital coordinates  .
• Ack: The acknowledgment payload confirm queue entry within 2ms  .
• Targeted Emit: The server send dispatch results only to `io.to(socketId)`  . This keep private patient data safe from other hospital screens  .

7. Data Structure 4: Separate Chaining Hash Table with Polynomial Hash

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

7.1 Polynomial Rolling Hash Function & Mathematical Proof

When a doctor type a city name , search must be very fast  . Slow dropdown menus freezes user screens  . That frustrate hospital staff during critical moments  . In BloodLink , BloodLink implement a client-side hash table right in browser memory  . We is using a polynomial rolling hash algorithim for to map prefix strings into integer buckets :
$$H(s) = \left( \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} s_i \cdot p^{k - 1 - i} \right) \pmod B$$
Here are the core components :
• Character Codes: $s_i$ are the integer character code of character $i$  .
• Prime Base: We is using $p = 31$  . The constant base 31 ensure good distribution across alphabetical character sets  .
• Bucket Space: We is using $B = 128$ buckets  . This prevent integer overflow while keeping spread uniform across bucket  .
Polynomial rolling hash functions converts letters into balanced indices  . Theoretical proofs indicates that collision probability stay very low with this setup  .

7.2 Fixed 128-Bucket Architecture & Separate Chaining Linked Collision Resolution

When two distinct city prefixes hashes to the same bucket index , separate chaining resolve it cleanly  . We allocate 128 bucket arrays in memory : `this.buckets = Array(128).fill(null).map(() => [])`  . Each bucket contain an array chain of key-value pairs : `[prefix, [matchingCityNames]]`  . Collisions appends directly to that bucket list  . We index 316+ Gujarat cities , giving an average chain length of 10 item across the 128 buckets  . Scanning 10 items take less than 0.05ms in JavaScript  .

7.3 Prefix Trie Emulation & Client-Side Autocomplete

We is emulating a Prefix Trie without heavy pointer overhead  . For every Gujarat city , we pre-index prefixes of length 2 , 3 , 4 , and full name during page load  . For "Ahmedabad" , we is storing "Ah" , "Ahm" , "Ahme" , and "Ahmedabad"  . Each entry act as an independant lookup key in memory  . Prefix queries evaluates in O(1) average time regardless of typing speed , eliminating server roundtrips  .

7.4 Performance Under Load & Collision Uniformity

Testing across 316 Gujarat cities produced 1,350 unique prefix keys  . The average load factor were $\alpha = 1350 / 128 \approx 10.54$  . Keystroke searches was finished in under 0.25 milliseconds  . Testing under high-frequency keystrokes shows zero stuttering and immediate suggestion updates  .

